# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-08-18, found on the live domain minutes after
# a release, by comparing what the browser had cached against what the
# server was sending):
#
# Pages serves JS with `max-age=14400` — four hours. On release night that
# gave returning visitors new HTML with stale MODULES, and the fix was to
# put a ?v= stamp on every /v2/ adapter import. The /calcv2/ ENGINE never
# got the same treatment, and the engine is the half that does the physics.
#
# It stayed invisible until a release changed engine BEHAVIOUR. This one
# did. Measured live: a returning browser was holding a 24,580-byte
# dynamicsCore.js with no PROPULSION_CHAIN_EFF and no liftVolumeM3, while
# the server sent the 30,956-byte version with both. New adapters, old
# physics — the DYNAMICS page read 58.4 t of LH2 instead of 74.9 t. The
# error was exactly 1/0.78, the propulsion-chain efficiency the cached
# engine had never heard of, and it was wrong in the FLATTERING direction,
# which is the worst way for a number on this site to be wrong.
#
# WHY A HEADER AND NOT MORE ?v= STAMPS. Stamping the five engine imports
# in dynamic-page.js was the first attempt and it is not sufficient — it is
# actively worse. The engine's modules import EACH OTHER with bare relative
# paths (cdEstimator.js does `import { ittcCf } from './dynamicsCore.js'`),
# and a relative import does not inherit its parent's query string. So a
# stamped entry point loads dynamicsCore.js?v=N while cdEstimator loads the
# unstamped, possibly-stale dynamicsCore.js — TWO instances of one module,
# one of them old, with no way to tell from the outside. Stamping every
# internal import instead would mean editing the canonical CalcV2 source
# the gate holds byte-identical, to solve a delivery problem in the browser.
#
# A header fixes every module, including transitive ones, at once and for
# good. must-revalidate with max-age=0 means the browser still caches the
# bytes and still gets a cheap 304 when nothing changed — this costs a
# conditional request, not a re-download.
#
# The /v2/ adapters keep their ?v= stamps. Belt and braces, and those
# stamps also document which release last touched each module.

/calcv2/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The adapters are stamped, but a stale one is just as fatal as a stale
# engine and the stamps have been forgotten before. Same treatment.
/v2/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
